1. FAISS CORE ARCHITECTURE & INDEX SELECTION

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

1.1 What is FAISS?

FAISS (Facebook AI Similarity Search) is a C++ library (with Python bindings) from Meta AI for efficient similarity search and clustering of dense vectors. It supports CPU and GPU execution and scales from thousands to billions of vectors.

Core operation: given database vectors x_i of dimension d, FAISS builds an index. When query vector x arrives, find argmin ||x - x_i|| (L2) or argmax <x, x_i> (inner product).

1.2 Index Lifecycle

  Train → Add → Search

  Train: Learn cluster centroids / codebooks (IVF, PQ indexes). Flat/HNSW skip this.
  Add: Insert database vectors. IVF assigns each to nearest cluster.
  Search: Given query vectors, return k nearest neighbors + distances.

1.3 Index Types — Selection Guide

  ┌──────────────────┬────────────┬──────────┬───────────────────┬────────────┬────────────┬───────────┐
  │ Index Type        │ factory    │ Training │ Memory/Vector     │ Recall     │ Speed      │ GPU?      │
  ├──────────────────┼────────────┼──────────┼───────────────────┼────────────┼────────────┼───────────┤
  │ Flat (Brute Force)│ Flat       │ No       │ d × 4 bytes       │ 100%       │ O(N)       │ Yes       │
  │ IVF-Flat          │ IVF{K},Flat│ Yes      │ d × 4 + overhead  │ High       │ Fast       │ Yes       │
  │ IVF-PQ            │ IVF{K},PQ{m}│ Yes    │ m bytes/vector    │ Good       │ Very fast  │ Yes       │
  │ HNSW              │ HNSW{M}   │ No       │ d×4 + M×8 bytes   │ Very High  │ Very fast  │ No (CPU)  │
  │ CAGRA (cuVS)      │ GpuCagra  │ Yes (GPU)│ GPU VRAM          │ Very High  │ Fastest GPU│ GPU only  │
  │ IVFPQFastScan     │ IVF,PQ4fs │ Yes      │ m/2 bytes/vector  │ Good       │ Fastest CPU│ No        │
  └──────────────────┴────────────┴──────────┴───────────────────┴────────────┴────────────┴───────────┘

1.4 Decision Tree

  Q: Few searches (< 10K queries)?         → Flat
  Q: Need exact results?                   → Flat
  Q: Memory NOT a concern?                 → HNSW (CPU) or CAGRA (GPU)
  Q: Memory somewhat constrained?          → IVF + Flat
  Q: Memory quite constrained?             → IVF + PQ
  Q: Maximum compression needed?           → RaBitQ (1 bit/dim)
  Q: Dataset < 1M?                         → IVF with K = 4√N to 16√N
  Q: Dataset 1M–10M?                       → IVF65536_HNSW32
  Q: Dataset 10M–100M?                     → IVF262144_HNSW32
  Q: Dataset > 100M?                       → IVF1048576_HNSW32

  For dedicated GPU capacity + need 100% recall:
  → Exhaustive kNN via cuBLAS matrix multiply (LinkedIn's approach)

1.5 Key Parameters

  d        = Dimensionality of vectors (64–1536, model dependent)
  nlist    = Number of IVF clusters (4√N to 16√N)
  nprobe   = Clusters searched at query time (1–256; higher = better recall, slower)
  M (HNSW) = Links per node (4–64; higher = more RAM, better recall)
  efSearch  = Search beam width for HNSW (16–512)
  m (PQ)   = Sub-quantizers for Product Quantization (8–96, each = 1 byte)

1.6 Distance Metrics

  L2 (Euclidean):  ||x - y||² — standard for general embeddings
  Inner Product:    <x, y>     — equivalent to cosine similarity after L2 normalization
  Cosine:           Normalize vectors to unit length, then use IP

