2. GPU ACCELERATION — FAISS-GPU, cuVS & CAGRA

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

2.1 Architecture

  FAISS GPU offloads distance computations to CUDA cores. Uses cuBLAS for 
  matrix multiplication. Supports: GpuIndexFlat, GpuIndexIVFFlat, 
  GpuIndexIVFPQ, GpuIndexCagra (via cuVS).

2.2 cuVS Integration (Since FAISS v1.10.0)

  cuVS (CUDA Vector Search) — NVIDIA's GPU-accelerated ANN library built on 
  RAPIDS RAFT. Integrated as optional backend.

  Key Benefits:
  • Build indexes up to 12× faster at 95% recall
  • Search latencies up to 8× lower at 95% recall
  • CPU↔GPU interoperability (build GPU, serve CPU)
  • CAGRA: GPU-native graph index — 4.7× faster than CPU HNSW
  • RMM pooled memory management

2.3 CAGRA — GPU-Native Graph Index

  Algorithm:
  1. Build kNN graph using IVF-PQ or NN-Descent
  2. Prune redundant routes → fixed-degree flat graph
  
  Key property: Can serialize to HNSW format for CPU search
  → Build fast on GPU, serve on CPU (hybrid deployment)

  USE CASE: Siri cloud entity search — build CAGRA on GPU, optionally
  convert to HNSW for on-device CPU fallback.

2.4 Exhaustive kNN on GPU (LinkedIn's Approach)

  When: Dedicated GPU capacity + corpus < ~100M + freshness matters

  Implementation: Q × D^T matrix multiply via cuBLAS
  • Q = [batch_size × d] query embeddings
  • D = [corpus_size × d] document embeddings
  • Result = [batch_size × corpus_size] similarity scores
  • Top-K via partial sort (cuDNN/custom kernel)

  WHY LinkedIn chose this over ANN:
  • 100% recall (no approximation loss)
  • Trivial index updates (replace embedding row)
  • No index rebuild for freshness
  • GPU tensor cores make brute-force fast enough

